WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: ar

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.760
Do you remember when I told you that I want to transfer 2 bitcoins to a friend

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:07.000
I shall ask him for his address in order to transfer them?

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:10.800
Ok, Do you remember when I said that I am the only one who can conduct my currencies as well

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:14.360
I am the only one who has the private key?

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:18.760
The question here is how transferring and receiving are being done?

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:22.320
Viz; How can I sell cryptocurrency and where do I keep them?

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:25.320
How can I keep them from being lost?

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:28.080
We will answer all of those questions today

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:34.000
I'm Ashraf Ibrahim, and this is our eighth episode of our new series on cryptocurrency "Crypto Economic Informant".

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:55.720
Whenever you need to buy anything from any market, You shall be keeping your money with you

00:00:55.720 --> 00:01:01.080
either in the form of cash in your wallet or in your bank account to pay by your credit card

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:04.640
Ok, if we apply the same idea to the cryptocurrencies you have;

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:06.520
what will happen if you want to buy something with these currencies?

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:10.280
Where would these currencies be? Where are they located or kept?

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:16.480
As said before; the bitcoin or other cryptocurrencies are virtual and intangible currencies

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:22.400
You can keep these cryptocurrencies in what are called crypto wallets.

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:24.480
What are crypto wallets and what are their types?

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:27.800
What are the most popular applications currently available for this wallet?

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:32.800
With the widespread use of cryptocurrencies, it was necessary to simulate traditional wallets,

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:37.280
however, not all cryptocurrency users are familiar with the world of programming.

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:44.440
It is hard for them to log into the blockchain to type the different codes and symbols for the transferring process to take place.

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:47.360
The idea of crypto-encrypted wallets emerged at that point.

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:50.320
These wallets are divided into two main types

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:53.880
The first type is the Hot wallet and the second type is the Cold wallet

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:58.880
Today we will talk about the first type, the Hot wallet, which is considered the most widely used.

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:02.840
It is an application with a simple and easy user interface.

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:06.600
It is called GUI or graphical user interface.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:10.120
The function of this type of wallet is to save the private key

00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:13.440
through which you can control your encrypted currencies

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:16.680
and sign the transactions, as we will explain later.

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:19.800
With the increasing popularity and momentum of cryptocurrencies in the world,

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:26.160
various types of hot wallets began to appear, most of them are free to download.

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:28.720
There are wallets that are downloaded directly to the computer,

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:30.720
such as Exodus and Electrum programs.

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:33.720
In addition to other wallets that can be downloaded as add-ons to any browser

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:36.320
such as Meta Mask and Binance Chain Wallet.

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:39.000
There are wallets that can be downloaded to the mobile as well,

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.000
such as the Trust Wallet and Safepal applications.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:44.120
After all these types had appeared, the process became easy and simple.

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:49.360
Here, of course, a question popped up: Are these wallets safe so that I can keep my crypto-currencies?

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:53.600
Is there a possibility that I may lose my wallet and subsequently my crypto-coins at any time?

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:55.960
In fact, no. These wallets are not safe enough,

00:02:55.960 --> 00:02:59.640
mainly because they are always connected to the internet.

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:03.480
Thereby, they are always an easy target for phishing attacks from hackers

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:08.240
who would try in different ways to access the private key of your wallet

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:11.760
so that they can control the wallet and the currencies in it.

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:14.760
The methods of circumvention among hackers are various.

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:20.200
For example, One might create a domain with the same name as the site you use

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:25.960
to log into your wallet asking you to update your data;

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:31.000
once you do, the hacker receives your data and seizes all your currencies.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:36.120
There is another popular scam; pretty like logging in to Facebook, for example,

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:41.560
a code will be sent to you in a short message on your mobile to verify that you are the owner of the account.

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:46.160
The same applies to wallets when you try to log in.

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:52.160
Some scammers have devised a way to get the code that connects you through a technique called “SIM swap”.

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:56.400
They communicate with telecom service providers and convince them

00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:00.080
that they are the owner of the SIM and ask them to replace the SIM

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:03.400
with a new one to use in the authentication step.

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:06.880
This is the same thing that happened in 2018 with Michael Terpin,

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:10.400
when he accused the American telecommunications company AT&amp;T

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:13.440
that it could not protect him from being defrauded

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:17.960
after nearly $24 million in cryptocurrencies were stolen from him.

00:04:17.960 --> 00:04:22.720
Ok, away from theft and fraud, is it possible to lose your wallet in another way?

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:29.680
Unfortunately, yes. You may not be able to access your wallet ever again if you lose the private key or even part of it.

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:31.840
Do you remember episode number 5 in the series,

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:35.440
in which we talked about wallet hunters, you can watch it through here,

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:39.480
which is why crypto experts always advise that one should make

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:43.760
more than one copy of the private key and keep them in separate places.

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:46.040
Here comes an important question, attention please:

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:50.440
How can I choose the right wallet for me among all these types?

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:55.360
Actually; to choose the right wallet any one ought to take care of the following:

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:58.760
Safety first… Before you deal with any wallet,

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:02.036
you must make sure that you downloaded it from the wallet’s website.

00:05:02.036 --> 00:05:05.120
The developers in charge are trusted.

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:08.440
Of course, it is preferable that the programming of the wallet be open source

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:13.680
for the users who have a technical background can check the source code,

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:19.240
identify the breaking points and share them with those in charge of developing the wallet to fill possible gaps.

00:05:19.240 --> 00:05:22.640
Second, the wallet must be supporting different cryptocurrencies.

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:25.000
Viz, during traditional currencies exchange,

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:28.000
if you want to transfer an amount of money in euros;

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:30.400
you cannot transfer it to a dollar account.

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:34.040
It must be a euro account in order to be able to receive it.

00:05:34.040 --> 00:05:37.800
The same applies in cryptocurrencies. It is not possible to transfer bitcoins

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:41.360
to a private address on the Ethereum network [for example] and vice versa.

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:46.640
You must first make sure that the wallet you will use supports your cryptocurrency

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:50.960
as there are wallets that do not support all types of currencies and their algorithms.

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:55.520
That is why we recommend that you use a wallet that contains most of the blockchain networks

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:59.760
and the currencies based on them, so that it would be easy for you to use.

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:03.400
The third thing, which is crucial, is technical support.

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:08.200
Sometimes a certain problem occurs when you transfer cryptocurrencies from a wallet to another.

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:13.560
The process is not complete, for example, or the wallet shows an error message or any other problems.

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:18.400
You must make sure that the wallet you will use has a strong support team

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:22.000
to communicate with and help you in case you are facing a problem.

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:26.440
My last advice is of significance too, please don't use the Hot wallet constantly.

00:06:26.440 --> 00:06:31.480
use it when you only want to buy something or transfer currencies to another wallet.

00:06:31.480 --> 00:06:37.520
But if you have coins to invest and save for a while, or if you have a large amount of crypto-coins.

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:44.440
You must use a cold wallet, which we will talk about in the next episode, God willing. So, stay tuned.

00:06:44.440 --> 00:06:47.000
This was a quick review on crypto-wallets and their types.

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:49.280
I advise you not to miss any episode of the series

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:52.440
to fully comprehend the basics of the cryptocurrency world.

